:: suitor_date_vaela_1 [suitor-date vaela]

The Iron Garden.

That was what the castle staff called the training yard — a name that married the stone enclosure's brutal functionality with the garden's promise that things could grow here, even if what grew was skill rather than roses. The flagstones were scarred by a thousand practice bouts. The weapons racks lined the eastern wall like metal ribs. The air smelled of sand and sweat and oiled steel.

Lady Vaela Rhyss did not wait.

<<passage_img "character/vaela/vaela_4.png">><</passage_img>>

She was already there when I arrived — already stripped to a fitted ivory-and-gold training tunic that hugged the lean, defined musculature of her frame with a frankness that was half martial, half provocative. Dark hair bound in a warrior's tail that fell between her shoulder blades like a whip. Skin pale and luminous, already flushed with the morning's heat. And eyes — golden-amber eyes that tracked my approach with the focused intensity of a predator assessing prey.

She did not bow. She did not greet me. She did not smile.

Two practice rapiers rested on the weapons rack. She took one, tested its balance with a flick of her wrist that made the blade sing, and threw the other one to me.

I caught it. Barely. The handle slapped into my palm with a *crack* that stung, and my fingers closed around the grip with the reflexive quickness that Aldric's training had beaten into muscle memory.

"I did not come to Valmoria to drink tea and discuss lineage," Vaela said. Her voice was low, resonant, carrying the accent of the Dawncourt — vowels rounded like river stones, consonants that cut like the blade she held. "I came to see if the heir is worth protecting."

She raised the rapier. The point aimed at my sternum with the casual precision of a woman who could thread a needle at twenty paces.

"Show me."

[[Fight|suitor_date_vaela_2]]


:: suitor_date_vaela_2 [suitor-date vaela]

She moved first.

The attack came in a form I had never encountered — a lateral slash that originated not from the shoulder, as I expected, but from the hip. The blade arced upward in a whipping crescent, powered by a rotational force that used the wider female pelvis as a fulcrum rather than the squared male stance I had been raised in. The Dawncourt Blade Form.

I parried. Barely. The impact rang up my forearm and into my shoulder, and the force of it surprised me — not the raw power, which was less than Aldric's strikes, but the efficiency. Every ounce of her body weight was channelled through that rotation. Nothing wasted. Nothing held in reserve.

She pressed. A thrust, a feint, a pivot that carried her around my guard before I could adjust, and suddenly her blade was at my ribs, the point dimpling the linen of the tunic-dress.

"Reset," she said. The word was not a command. It was an invitation. *Come again. Do better.*

I set the feminine guard — feet shoulder-width, weight on the balls, rapier extended at mid-line, the hip-driven stance Aldric had been teaching me. It felt raw still, unfinished, a technique my body was learning to inhabit rather than one it owned.

Vaela saw it. Her golden eyes narrowed — not with contempt, but with recognition.

"The Vornhelm guard," she said. "Adapted for a changing frame." She shifted her own stance — a mirror of mine, but perfected. Hips open, weight settled, the rapier an extension of her spine. "He taught you the bones of it. I shall teach you the blood."

She attacked.

The second bout lasted longer — perhaps ninety seconds. I managed three parries, a cross-step that Aldric would have approved of, and a riposte that Vaela deflected with a flick of the wrist so economical it barely qualified as movement. She fought the way a river fights — flowing around obstacles, finding gaps, never forcing, always adapting. Every strike flowed from her centre, powered by the same biomechanics that I was trying to learn, and watching her was like watching a proof of concept — a living demonstration that the feminine body was not a limitation but an architecture, and she had built a fighting style that used every curve and angle as load-bearing structure.

On my fourth parry, she disarmed me. A bind, a twist, and the rapier was wrenched from my grip and clattering across the flagstones. Her blade hovered at my throat — close enough that the cool steel kissed the skin beneath my jaw.

She grinned.

Not cruelly. Not mockingly. The fierce, bright-eyed grin of a warrior who has found an opponent worth the effort — one who held for ninety seconds when most would have folded in thirty.

"Again."

[[Spar again|suitor_date_vaela_3]]


:: suitor_date_vaela_3 [suitor-date vaela]

We sparred three more times.

Each bout, I lasted longer. The feminine guard that Aldric had planted in my body began to sprout — the hip-driven rotation that Vaela used instinctively, I was learning to adopt through repetition, through observation, through the primal teacher of a blade at my throat and the refusal to let it stay there.

The second bout: forty seconds before disarming. The cross-step was cleaner. The pivot-retreat — that backward glide that dropped beneath a horizontal slash — came without conscious thought. Vaela nodded when she disarmed me. A nod that said *better.*

The third bout: two minutes. I found the rotation. Not perfectly — the hips were still learning their new proportions, still arguing with the muscle memory of a male pelvis — but enough to power a riposte that came from the core rather than the shoulder, and the blade moved faster because the engine driving it was more efficient. Vaela's eyes widened by a fraction when the riposte nearly tagged her forearm.

"There," she breathed. "That. Again."

The fourth bout.

I set the guard. Vaela circled. The morning sun was high enough now to pour into the Iron Garden, catching the dust motes we kicked up from the flagstones, gilding the sweat on her arms and the steel of the practice blades. Her dark hair had loosened from the warrior's tail, strands clinging to her neck and jaw, and her breathing was deeper now — not ragged, but present, the rhythm of a body working near its edge.

She attacked. The lateral slash — her signature opening, the hip-driven crescent. I read it this time. Shifted my weight to the rear foot, let the slash pass through the space where my ribs had been a heartbeat earlier, and drove forward into the gap her rotation created.

The rapier's point tagged her shoulder.

The practice blade kissed the ivory fabric of her training tunic — not hard enough to mark, barely hard enough to register — and we both froze.

Vaela looked down at the spot where my blade had touched her.

Then she laughed.

A real laugh — not the controlled, courtly sound I had heard from the other suitors during the ceremony, but something bright and wild and unguarded, a laugh that came from the belly and the chest and the deep place where exhilaration and violence met. It rang off the Iron Garden's stone walls and scattered upward into the morning sky like startled birds.

"There it is," she said, her golden eyes finding mine, her voice carrying the raw warmth of someone who had found exactly what they came looking for. "The queen."

[[Continue|suitor_date_vaela_4]]


:: suitor_date_vaela_4 [suitor-date vaela]

The aftermath was charged.

We stood perhaps three feet apart. Both breathing hard — my chest heaving against the budding tissue with each inhale, the tenderness that was my constant companion now sharpened by exertion into something that bordered on painful. Sweat ran down my temples and traced the line of my jaw — the softer jawline, the one that was reshaping itself week by week beneath the alchemy's patient hand — and darkened the collar of the cream tunic-dress.

Vaela was sweating too. Her ivory training tunic clung to her frame, the fabric translucent at the shoulders and the small of her back, and the body beneath was a sculptor's study in controlled power — lean muscle over long bones, a warrior's shoulders above a dancer's waist, the swell of breasts held firm by the tunic's fitted cut. She wore her body the way Aldren wore his armour: naturally, completely, without apology.

My hair had escaped the braid during the sparring. Dark waves fell past my shoulders — longer than I realised, the transformation accelerating the growth — and they were damp and tangled and clinging to my neck in ways that my hair had never done before because there had never been enough of it.

Vaela reached out.

She tucked a strand behind my ear.

The touch was light. Fingertips against the side of my face — just above the ear, where the skin was thin and sensitive. Her fingers were calloused from the sword grip but warm, and they lingered against my temple for a beat longer than the gesture required, tracing the curve of my ear, grazing the jaw that was softening day by day beneath flesh that remembered a different shape.

My breath stopped.

Not a decision. Not a choice. A purely physical response — the lungs seizing, the heartbeat slamming forward into a sudden, accelerating rhythm that pulsed through the new tissue at my chest with aching clarity. Each heartbeat was a drum strike against flesh that had not existed three months ago, and the sensitivity was maddening, amplifying every sensation until Vaela's fingertips against my jaw felt like they were touching nerve endings that ran all the way to the base of my spine.

Her golden eyes held mine. This close, I could see the flecks of darker amber in the irises, the fine lines at the corners where sun and wind and laughter had left their marks. The pupils were wide. Not with exertion — we had stopped moving. With something else.

"You are caught between," she murmured. Her voice was low, barely above a whisper, and her breath was warm against my face — we were that close, though I could not remember either of us stepping forward. "I have never wanted anything more."

[[Continue|suitor_date_vaela_5]]


:: suitor_date_vaela_5 [suitor-date vaela]

She pulled me forward.

Her hand closed on the collar of the tunic-dress — not roughly, but with the decisive grip of a woman who did not ask permission to take what she wanted because she had never needed to — and drew me toward her until our faces were inches apart.

I could see every detail of her. The thin scar that bisected her left eyebrow — a sparring wound, or something worse. The faint sheen of sweat on her upper lip. The way her nostrils flared slightly with each breath. The dark hair that had escaped the warrior's tail, framing her face in damp tendrils that clung to the pale skin.

I could feel the heat coming off her. Her body radiated warmth the way a forge radiates heat — concentrated, intense, the product of sustained effort and a metabolism that ran hot. The air between us was thick with it — her warmth, my warmth, the iron tang of sweat and the mineral scent of the flagstones and something beneath both that was purely chemical, purely animal, a scent I had never noticed before because the body I was becoming processed sensation differently than the body I had been.

My heart slammed against the budding flesh of my chest. Each beat sent a pulse of sensation through the tender tissue — not pain, exactly, but a heightened awareness that bordered on it, a sensitivity so acute that I could feel my own pulse in places that had not existed before, in the swelling curves beneath the linen, in the thin skin that stretched tighter with each passing day over growth that would not be denied.

Vaela's mouth was close enough that I could feel her breath on my lips.

For a breathless, suspended moment, we hovered. The Iron Garden was silent. The morning sun poured down like molten gold. The practice rapiers lay forgotten on the flagstones. And I was aware — with a clarity that was almost violent — that every nerve in this half-finished body was reaching toward her.

Vaela released.

She opened her hand and stepped back. One step, then two, creating distance with the deliberate control of a woman who was choosing not to take something she wanted very badly.

She smiled. Not the fierce grin of the sparring — something else. Something slower, warmer, more dangerous. A wolf's smile. Patient, knowing, hungry.

"Not yet," she said. "I want you to choose me, not fall into me."

She turned. Walked to the weapons rack. Retrieved both rapiers and replaced them with a precision that was almost meditative, each blade settled into its bracket with a soft *click* of steel on wood.

Then she walked away. Her hips moved with a fighter's economy — tight, controlled, powerful — but there was something else in the motion now. An awareness. A sway that was not calculated but was not accidental either, the natural rhythm of a woman's body in movement, and it pulled my gaze down her spine and along the curve of her waist and lower, and I could not look away.

She did not look back.

[[Continue|suitor_date_vaela_6]]


:: suitor_date_vaela_6 [suitor-date vaela]

I stood in the Iron Garden.

The flagstones were warm beneath my boots. The sun was directly overhead now, pouring heat into the stone enclosure, and the air shimmered above the practice ground in waves that made the walls seem to breathe.

My pulse was still thundering. Not the even, controlled rhythm that Aldric had trained into me — the wild, irregular stammer of a heart that had been ambushed by something it did not have a name for. Each beat resonated through my chest, through the budding flesh that the potions were building with relentless, aching patience, and the ghost of Vaela's fingers lingered on my jawline like a brand.

She had almost kissed me.

Or I had almost kissed her. The geometry of those final inches was ambiguous — who had leaned, who had tilted, whose breath had quickened first — and the ambiguity itself was maddening, because it meant that whatever had happened in the space between our mouths was shared. Mutual. A thing we had both reached for and both denied.

Vaela Rhyss. A woman. A warrior-goddess from the Dawncourt who fought with the Blade Form that used the female body as an engine rather than an obstacle. Who had disarmed me three times and praised me the fourth. Who had touched my face with sword-calloused fingers and said *I have never wanted anything more* while looking at a body that was neither one thing nor the other, a body caught between, and had wanted it precisely because of the between.

The word surfaced from somewhere deep. A word I had been circling for weeks without ever quite landing on it, the way you circle a hot coal barefoot — aware of the heat, unable to commit to the step.

*Desire.*

Not admiration. Not curiosity. Not the intellectual acknowledgment that Vaela was beautiful and powerful and interesting. Desire — the visceral, physical, aching pull of a body toward another body, the kind of want that lived below the collarbone and above the navel and had nothing to do with thought.

And the body that desired her was not the body of a prince. It was this body — the in-between one, the changing one, the one with budding breasts and softening jaw and widening hips. The body that was becoming female desired a woman with a ferocity that stole my breath and left me standing alone in the Iron Garden with sweat cooling on skin that no longer belonged to who I used to be.

My hand went to my jawline. Traced the spot where her fingers had been. The skin was still warm.

