She terrified me. I wanted more.

The two truths coexisted without contradiction, occupying the same space in my chest the way the new tissue occupied the same ribcage as the old muscle — pushing, pressing, making room for themselves in a body that had not planned for their arrival.

Vaela Rhyss terrified me because she did not handle me gently. She did not treat the transformation as a fragility to be accommodated. She had thrown a rapier at me and said *show me* — and when I showed her, she had seen the queen.

I wanted more because the place where her fingers had touched my jaw was still singing. Because the near-kiss hung in the air of the Iron Garden like a chord that had not resolved. Because the word *desire* had lodged itself beneath my ribs and was pulsing there with each heartbeat, warm and terrifying and undeniably alive.

I left the Iron Garden with my pulse still hammering and the smell of sweat and steel in my nostrils and the shape of Vaela's smile imprinted on the inside of my eyelids.

[[Report to Serelyne|suitor_date_evaluation_1]]


:: suitor_date_vaela_7b [suitor-date vaela choice]
<<set $vaela_affection -= 2>>

A skilled warrior. Undeniably talented. The Dawncourt Blade Form was a revelation — proof that the feminine body could fight not merely adequately but brilliantly, and the techniques Vaela had demonstrated in those four bouts would change the way I trained with Aldric.

But the touch. The collar grab. The whispered declaration and the near-kiss and the deliberate, theatrical withdrawal — it was too much. Too aggressive. The border between confidence and presumption was fine, and Vaela had crossed it with the same ease she crossed swords.

I left the Iron Garden appreciating what she had shown me with the blade and wary of what she had shown me without it.

[[Report to Serelyne|suitor_date_evaluation_1]]


:: suitor_date_vaela_7c [suitor-date vaela choice]

I felt nothing.

I told myself this as I left the Iron Garden. Felt nothing. The sparring was educational, the near-kiss was a power play, and the thundering of my pulse was simple exertion from four bouts of combat. Nothing more.

The ghost of her fingers on my jaw argued otherwise, but I had become skilled at ignoring the testimony of a body I no longer trusted to tell me the truth about anything.

[[Report to Serelyne|suitor_date_evaluation_1]]
